              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The complete quote is:\n\n> **\"...those owned by wealthy rulers.\"**\n\nThis famous statement is attributed to **Ibn Khaldun** (1332\u20131406), the influential 14th-century North African polymath, sociologist, and historian, from his seminal work ***The Muqaddimah*** (written in 1377).\n\n### Context and Meaning\nIn *The Muqaddimah*, Ibn Khaldun laid down early foundations for political economy, prefiguring modern free-market ideas (such as those of Adam Smith) by several centuries. \n\nWhen he made this observation, he was addressing the role of government in the economy. Ibn Khaldun argued that **rulers and governments should not engage in commerce** because state-run monopolies and commercial ventures ultimately stifle private enterprise. \n\nHis reasoning was rooted in several key economic observations:\n1. **The Inefficiency of State Power:** Rulers have the power of coercion, taxation, and regulation. When they enter the market, they inevitably use these powers to squeeze out competitors, dictate prices, and buy goods under market value. \n2. **Lack of Incentives:** Rulers do not face the same survival-of-the-fittest pressures as private merchants. They do not have to be organized, efficient, or responsive to market demands because they can always rely on the treasury or force to subsidize their losses.\n3. **The Discipline of the Merchant:** In contrast, private merchants must rely on foresight, strict organization, relationship-building, and careful risk management to survive. Because their livelihood depends entirely on their competence, they operate with a level of responsibility and diligence that the state cannot replicate.\n\nUltimately, Ibn Khaldun argued that while a wealthy ruler's business might look powerful initially due to state funding, it is unsustainable. Over time, the organic discipline and organizational superiorities of private, responsible merchants will always prevail.",
